IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Old Age Security Act (R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9) and Old Age Security Regulations (C.R.C., c. 1246).
-- THE CORE: the nouns that two or more pieces of the encoding share.
--
-- This module holds DECLAREs, one shim and nothing else. It states no rule of law; the rules
-- that work out the Act's defined terms are in the oas-core-* modules that import it.
-- Every piece of the encoding imports this module. A name declared here is the only declaration
-- of that name anywhere in the encoding: a piece never declares one of these again.
--
-- Source: the plain-text renderings in the deposit (R.S.C.-1985-c.-O-9.txt, C.R.C.-c.-1246.txt),
-- made by a converter script from the Justice Laws Website consolidation current to 2026-09-30.
-- They are not the official text. Quotations below are copied mechanically by srcrefs.py.
--
-- CONVENTIONS (the same text, in more words, is in NOTES.md under "Naming conventions"):
--   money          a NUMBER of Canadian dollars. Cents are a fraction (273.80). Never rounded
--                  unless the text says so; see `rounded to the nearest cent` in oas-core-measures.
--   percentage     a NUMBER that is a fraction of one: 0.6% is written 0.6% (the postfix operator),
--                  which is the NUMBER 0.006.
--   days, months, years, ages
--                  a NUMBER, the unit in the name of the thing: `in days`, `in months`, `in years`.
--                  An age is the NUMBER of complete years.
--   a date         a DATE made with Date day month year (day first) or with the shim YMD below.
--   a month        the DATE of its first day. `the first day of the month containing` turns any
--                  date into that. The Act pays and counts by the month, so "the month" is always
--                  this, never a month number typed in.
--   a time limit   a `Time limit`: a number of units and a unit (Days, Months or Years).
--   a rule input   a plain fact the person can state, or a fact taken from another piece; see
--                  NOTES.md "Instructions to the pieces".

-- YMD is missing from the stable prelude (build 1.6.98); this is the local shim the brief asks for.
-- It is the Date constructor with the arguments in year, month, day order. Like Date it is lenient:
-- an out-of-range day or month rolls over. Nothing else in the encoding may define YMD.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YMD y m d MEANS Date d m y

-- "benefit means a pension, supplement or allowance"
-- src:20 | benefit means a pension, supplement or allowance; (prestation)
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 2 "benefit" (src:20)
DECLARE Benefit IS ONE OF
    Pension
    Supplement
    Allowance

-- s 3(1) and 3(2): the two sizes of pension. The amounts are in section 7 and subsection 3(3).
-- src:96 | 3 (1) Subject to this Act and the regulations, a full monthly pension may be paid to
-- src:107 | (2) Subject to this Act and the regulations, a partial monthly pension may be paid for any month in a payment quarter to every person who is not eligible for a full monthly pension under subsection (1) and
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, ss 3(1), 3(2) (src:96) (src:107)
DECLARE `Kind of pension` IS ONE OF
    `Full monthly pension`
    `Partial monthly pension`

-- ss 19 and 21: the two allowances. Section 20 and the Regulations call them by these section numbers.
-- src:515 | 19 (1) Subject to this Act and the regulations, an allowance may be paid to the spouse, common-law partner or former common-law partner of a pensioner for a month in a payment period if the spouse, common-law partner or former common-law partner, as the case may be,
-- src:573 | 21 (1) Subject to this Act and the regulations, for each month in any payment period, an allowance may be paid to a survivor who
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, ss 19(1), 21(1) (src:515) (src:573)
DECLARE `Kind of allowance` IS ONE OF
    `Allowance under section 19`
    `Allowance under section 21`

-- The persons and bodies the Act and the Regulations name as doing or receiving something.
-- Only the Minister is defined (s 2). The rest are named where they act; the sections are listed
-- so that a piece can see where each comes from. A regulative rule uses Party as its PARTY type.
-- src:57 | Minister means the Minister of Employment and Social Development; (ministre)
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 2 "Minister" (src:57)
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    Minister                                         -- s 2: "the Minister of Employment and Social Development"
    `Governor in Council`                            -- ss 34, 34.2 (regulations)
    Applicant                                        -- s 2
    Beneficiary                                      -- s 2
    Pensioner                                        -- s 2
    Survivor                                         -- s 2
    `Spouse or common-law partner`                   -- s 2 "common-law partner"; ss 19(1), 19(6)(d), 28(2): of an applicant, a pensioner or a beneficiary
    `Former common-law partner`                      -- s 19(1)
    Sponsor                                          -- ss 11(7)(e), 19(6)(d), 21(9)(c); Regs 22.1(1)
    Representative                                   -- Regs 4(1) "a responsible person or agency"; Regs 24(1) the person or agency appointed to receive a benefit
    Estate                                           -- ss 29(1), 29(2), 37(2.1) "estate or succession"; Regs 24(5)
    `Social Security Tribunal`                       -- s 28(1); Regs 38
    `Tax Court of Canada`                            -- s 28(2); Regs 2(1) "Court"
    `Registrar of the Tax Court of Canada`           -- Regs 38.1(2), 39(1), 40(1)
    `Federal Court`                                  -- ss 37(2), 37(2.01), 37(2.02); s 44.2(1) "judge"
    `Provincial or municipal authority`              -- s 36(2); Regs 4(2), 28.1
    `Statistics Canada`                              -- ss 2 "Consumer Price Index", 6, 27
    `Minister of National Revenue`                   -- s 33.1; Regs 17
    `Other person`                                   -- "A person who has received ..." (s 37(1)), "Every person who ..." (s 44(1)): anyone not listed above

-- A stretch of days, both ends included. The payment period, the payment quarter and the two
-- adjustment quarters of s 2 are Calendar spans, and so are the base calendar year of ss 10 and 22
-- and the current payment period.
-- src:58 | payment period, in relation to a month, means
-- src:62 | payment quarter means a period of three months commencing on the first day of April, July, October or January in a payment period; (trimestre de paiement)
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, s 2 "payment period", "payment quarter" (src:58) (src:62)
DECLARE `Calendar span` HAS
    `first day` IS A DATE
    `last day`  IS A DATE

-- The one way a time limit is written (a convention of this encoding; no provision licenses it).
-- The text states its limits in days ("within ninety days", s 27.1(1)), in months ("eleven months
-- before", s 11(7)(a)) and in years ("within one year", s 29(1)). Each is a Time limit; the rules
-- `the day that is ... after` and `the day that is ... before` in oas-core-measures turn one and a
-- start date into a date, counting months and years on the calendar.
DECLARE `Unit of time` IS ONE OF
    Days
    Months
    Years

DECLARE `Time limit` HAS
    `number of units` IS A NUMBER
    unit              IS A `Unit of time`

-- A sentence of imprisonment, with the facts that decide whether the person counts as "an incarcerated
-- person described in subsection 5(3)". Subsection 5(3)(a) and (b), paragraphs 19(6)(f) and 21(9)(d)
-- repeat the same two limbs, so the three share this record; each provision keeps its own rule.
-- src:131 | (3) No pension may be paid in respect of a period of incarceration — exclusive of the first month of that period — to a person who is subject to a sentence of imprisonment
-- src:132 | (a) that is to be served in a penitentiary by virtue of any Act of Parliament; or
-- src:133 | (b) that exceeds 90 days and is to be served in a prison, as defined in subsection 2(1) of the Prisons and Reformatories Act, if the government of the province in which the prison is located has entered into an agreement under section 41 of the Department of Employment and Social Development Act.
-- src:549 | (f) any period of incarceration — exclusive of the first month of that period — during which the spouse or common-law partner is subject to a sentence of imprisonment
-- src:550 | (i) that is to be served in a penitentiary by virtue of any Act of Parliament, or
-- src:551 | (ii) that exceeds 90 days and is to be served in a prison, as defined in subsection 2(1) of the Prisons and Reformatories Act, if the government of the province in which the prison is located has entered into an agreement under section 41 of the Department of Employment and Social Development Act.
-- src:607 | (d) any period of incarceration — exclusive of the first month of that period — during which the survivor is subject to a sentence of imprisonment
-- src:608 | (i) that is to be served in a penitentiary by virtue of any Act of Parliament, or
-- src:609 | (ii) that exceeds 90 days and is to be served in a prison, as defined in subsection 2(1) of the Prisons and Reformatories Act, if the government of the province in which the prison is located has entered into an agreement under section 41 of the Department of Employment and Social Development Act.
@ref R.S.C., 1985, c. O-9, ss 5(3), 19(6)(f), 21(9)(d) (src:131-133) (src:549-551) (src:607-609)
DECLARE `Sentence of imprisonment` HAS
    `to be served in a penitentiary by virtue of an Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
    `length in days` IS A NUMBER
    `to be served in a prison as defined in subsection 2(1) of the Prisons and Reformatories Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the government of the province in which the prison is located has entered into an agreement under section 41 of the Department of Employment and Social Development Act` IS A BOOLEAN
